What:           /sys/class/net/<interface>/tx_diagnostics
Date:           September 2026
KernelVersion:  7.1
Contact:        Linux wireless developers <linux-wireless@vger.kernel.org>
Description:
                Read-only (0444) versioned snapshot of the MT6797 radio's
                software transmit queue and send worker. Available only on
                the cfg80211 radio netdev, for its registration lifetime.
                Reading does not reset counters or perform firmware access.

                ASCII key=value lines contain cumulative packet/event counts,
                queue depth/high-water, monotonic nanosecond durations and
                comma-separated duration histograms. version=1 identifies
                this format. hist_upper_ns gives inclusive upper bounds for
                every histogram, ending in an unbounded final bucket.
                lifetime_start_ns and connection_generation distinguish the
                counter lifetime and accepted connect requests. snapshot_ns
                identifies the coherent observation time.

                queue_wait measures accepted enqueue to worker dequeue;
                send_duration measures dequeue through submission/error and
                local packet release; credit_wait measures completion waits
                following a firmware transport credit shortage. worker_duration
                measures each complete TX worker invocation, including its
                connection-lock waits. Each has _count, _total_ns, _max_ns and
                _hist fields. Active states/ages describe incomplete spans.

                hif_submitted counts successful transport submissions, not
                firmware or over-the-air TX completion. credit_wait_wakeups
                similarly do not imply TX completion. No payload, key, SSID,
                peer/station address or per-packet identifier is exposed.

                All values share one radio-lock snapshot. Counters persist
                over interface stop and reconnect and disappear on netdev
                removal. There is no writable/reset attribute. Details and
                safe interval interpretation are documented in
                tools/gemini/README.wifi-tx-diagnostics.md.
